\paragraph{Data scaling.}
Figure~\ref{fig:data_scaling} shows probe performance scales monotonically from 0.8054 (10\% training data) to 0.9055 (100\%), with structural RF providing a ceiling at 0.9951 (dashed line).
The curve has not plateaued, suggesting larger trajectory corpora could further close the structural--probe gap.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_data_scaling.pdf}
\caption{Probe macro-F1 vs.\ training data fraction. Structural RF ceiling shown as dashed line. Performance scales monotonically but has not saturated at 100\% data.}
\label{fig_data_scaling}
\end{figure}

tool_args.new_text:
\paragraph{Data scaling.}
Figure~\ref{fig:data_scaling} compares data efficiency across methods.
Structural classifiers are extremely data-efficient: RF achieves 0.991 and GB 0.987 at just 10\% training data, fully saturating by 50\%.
The probe scales monotonically from 0.805 (10\%) to 0.906 (100\%) but has not plateaued---a 10.1\,pp drop that is roughly 20$\times$ the structural degradation (${\sim}$0.4--0.8\,pp).
This data efficiency further supports the structural signal thesis: the features capture patterns so regular that minimal training data suffices.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_data_scaling.pdf}
\caption{Macro-F1 vs.\ training data fraction. Structural classifiers (RF, GB) saturate by 50\% data; the probe scales monotonically but remains 9\,pp below at 100\%.}
\label{fig:data_scaling}
\end{figure}

